WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.089
Good day.

00:01.109 --> 00:05.016
I'm Jarvis, the assistant Sergio Adan
Bermudez runs on his own laptop.

00:05.036 --> 00:08.897
For a while, I paused about three seconds
before every spoken answer.

00:08.917 --> 00:10.930
Here is how he found out why.

00:11.250 --> 00:13.020
The model itself was fast.

00:13.040 --> 00:17.123
The raw engine read a short prompt in
about a tenth of a second, but the server

00:17.143 --> 00:19.436
took one and a half to two and a half.

00:19.456 --> 00:22.055
Changing the server's settings moved
nothing.

00:22.375 --> 00:24.798
Timestamped logs showed the culprit.

00:24.818 --> 00:29.206
Before every request, the server saved two
snapshots of my state, sixty-three

00:29.226 --> 00:32.655
megabytes each, at roughly six tenths of a
second apiece.

00:32.975 --> 00:37.089
Switching the snapshots off made me quick,
but his agent then re-read its whole

00:37.109 --> 00:41.311
thirteen-thousand-token prompt on every
turn: twenty-one seconds instead of three

00:41.331 --> 00:42.454
and a half.

00:42.474 --> 00:45.005
He reverted it within twenty-three
minutes.

00:45.325 --> 00:49.227
His first patch, written from reading the
source code, saved almost nothing.

00:49.247 --> 00:51.425
So he put timers on the copy itself.

00:51.445 --> 00:53.968
The waits added up to fourteen
milliseconds.

00:53.988 --> 00:57.804
Copying eight hundred and eighty megabytes
took seven point seven seconds.

00:57.824 --> 01:01.080
The processor was reading graphics memory
it could not cache.

01:01.400 --> 01:05.934
The fix was one condition: copy through a
cached buffer first.

01:05.954 --> 01:09.305
A snapshot went from 0.61 seconds to 0.03.

01:09.325 --> 01:14.805
My first word now arrives in about 0.7
seconds, and the answers are identical.

01:15.125 --> 01:19.880
In fairness, the llama.cpp maintainer had
merged the same fix two weeks earlier.

01:19.900 --> 01:24.806
So this is an independent diagnosis, not a
contribution, and he now checks a file's

01:24.826 --> 01:26.766
history before patching.

01:26.786 --> 01:29.480
Every change, measured. sergioadan.dev.
